      "source": "en_US",
      "content": "The reskinning tell in the other direction. Generated American English is often British English with the spelling changed, and the leftovers are formats and idiom rather than spelling: *color* and *organize* in a text that still writes *at the weekend*, *£*, *24 August*, and *Mr* without a period.\n\nThe corporate-American register a model defaults to when asked for American English: *leverage*, *unlock*, *supercharge*, *seamless*, *robust*, *best-in-class*, *move the needle*, *circle back*, *at the end of the day*, *double down*, *take it to the next level*. These arrive as a set rather than because any one of them was meant.\n\n*Utilize* for *use*, *incentivize* for *encourage*, *operationalize* for *do*. The longer verb is not the more precise one, and its density is a tell rather than a register.\n\nEm dash density. The unspaced em dash is correct American punctuation; what marks generated prose is one in every paragraph, used to attach an afterthought the sentence did not need — like this, repeatedly.\n\nHouse-style inconsistency within a document — the serial comma present in one list and absent in the next, *p.m.* in one time and *pm* in another — is a generation artefact rather than a choice, and worth naming as such rather than silently normalizing, because it usually means the passage came from somewhere else.\n\nThe listicle reflex in running prose: every paragraph opening with an ordinal or a bolded label, and every idea presented as a numbered takeaway whether or not the argument has parts."
